1. INTERVIEW READY ANSWERS
======================================================================

Q1. What is an Algorithm?

Answer:
An algorithm is a finite, well-defined, step-by-step procedure used to solve a specific problem. It takes input, processes it, produces the required output, and terminates after a finite number of steps.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q2. What are the characteristics of a good algorithm?

Answer:
• Input (0 or more inputs)
• Output (At least one output)
• Definiteness (Every step is clear and unambiguous)
• Finiteness (Terminates after finite steps)
• Effectiveness (Each instruction is executable)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Why do we analyze an algorithm?

Answer:
Algorithm analysis helps compare the efficiency of different algorithms based on time and space complexity. It is machine-independent and helps choose the most scalable solution.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Algorithm vs Program

Algorithm
• Language independent
• Describes WHAT to do
• Logical solution

Program
• Language dependent
• Describes HOW to do it
• Executable implementation

------------------------------------------------------------

Q5. What is Time Complexity?

Answer:
Time complexity measures how the number of operations performed by an algorithm grows as the input size (n) increases. It does not measure execution time in seconds.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q6. What is Space Complexity?

Answer:
Space complexity is the total memory required by an algorithm.

Space Complexity
=
Input Space
+
Auxiliary Space

Input Space
→ Memory required for input.

Auxiliary Space
→ Extra memory used by the algorithm.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Time Complexity vs Space Complexity

Time Complexity
• Measures operations
• Goal → Faster execution
• Unit → Operations

Space Complexity
• Measures memory
• Goal → Less memory usage
• Unit → Memory

------------------------------------------------------------

Q8. What is Big O Notation?

Answer:
Big O notation represents the upper bound (worst-case growth) of an algorithm. It describes how the running time grows as input size increases.

------------------------------------------------------------

Q9. Big O vs Big Ω vs Big Θ

Big O (O)
→ Upper Bound
→ Commonly used for Worst Case

Big Ω (Omega)
→ Lower Bound
→ Commonly used for Best Case

Big Θ (Theta)
→ Tight Bound
→ Exact asymptotic growth
(Not Average Case)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q10. Best, Average and Worst Case

Best Case
→ Minimum operations

Average Case
→ Expected operations over all possible inputs

Worst Case
→ Maximum operations

Example (Linear Search)

Best
→ Element at first position
→ O(1)

Average
→ Element somewhere in the middle
→ O(n)

Worst
→ Element at last position or absent
→ O(n)

------------------------------------------------------------

Q11. Why do we ignore constants and lower-order terms?

Answer:
Big O focuses on the growth rate of an algorithm for very large inputs. Constants and lower-order terms become insignificant as n increases.

Examples

3n + 5
→ O(n)

5n² + 2n + 10
→ O(n²)

